      <bioghist encodinganalog="545">
         <head>Biographical/Historical Information</head>
         <p>The Polygraph Examiners Advisory Board was established in 1968 to regulate individuals who administer and interpret lie detecting tests. The Department of Professional and Occupational Regulation provides administrative support to the board and the Director of the Department has full governing authority.</p>
      </bioghist>
      <odd encodinganalog="500">
         <p>These records are part of the Virginia Dept. of Professional and Occupational Regulation record group (RG 17)</p>
      </odd>


<odd encodinganalog="500">
<p>Electronic records in this collection are read-only and are only available in the Archives Research Room at the Library of Virginia.   A file list is available through the online catalog entry and the link below in the finding aid.  Copies are free of charge and may be requested while using the collection in-house. Copies may also be requested by contacting Archives Reference Services.  Processing will take from 4-8 weeks.</p>
</odd>



      <accessrestrict encodinganalog="506">
         <p>Meeting attachments must be reviewed for personal information and redacted prior to being served.</p>
      </accessrestrict>
      <prefercite encodinganalog="524">
         <head>Preferred Citation
		</head>
         <p>Minutes of the Virginia Polygraph Examiners Advisory Board, [cite specific date and accession used]. State government records collection, The Library of Virginia, Richmond, Va. 23219.</p>
      </prefercite>
      

      <scopecontent encodinganalog="520$a">
         <p>Minutes and related attachments documenting the meetings, activities, discussions, policy decisions, and recommendations of the Virginia Polygraph Examiners Advisory Board. Includes official board minutes, as well as draft minutes and agendas.  May also include informal fact finding hearing minutes, correspondence, legislation, regulations and reports.</p>



<p>Electronic records often contain board meeting packets with various attachments and draft minutes, while paper records often include minutes only. There may be some duplication between paper and electronic records. For transfers prior to 2012, records of individual boards were accessioned separately. After this, transfers of records (records starting in 2008) included all boards, which were accessioned under one accession number. There is a separate finding aid for the later transfers (link to finding aid below).</p>
      </scopecontent>



